schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Quadratic funding lets many small donors collectively outweigh a few large ones"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Quadratic funding (as used by Gitcoin Grants) amplifies small donations by
    matching them based on how many people contribute rather than how much
    each person gives, so many small donors collectively have more influence
    than a few large ones.
  evidence: >-
    Article cites Gitcoin Grants as the working example; general mechanism
    description, no specific match-pool figures cited.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/cooperative-commons.md
  interpretation: >-
    Presented as one of several viable funding paths for cooperative/commons
    work, alongside member contributions, savings-group formalization, and
    grants.
  uncertainty: >-
    No citation to a primary quadratic-funding explainer or dataset; mechanism
    description is accurate at a conceptual level but simplified (real QF
    implementations add sybil-resistance and collusion mitigations not
    mentioned here).
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/cooperative-commons.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-d800b62ea134
